  "attributionNote": "Anonymous, and part of the Katha school of the Krishna Yajurveda. Its teaching is given as a dialogue between the boy Nachiketas and Yama, the lord of the dead.",
  "description": "Nachiketas is offered three boons by Death and asks for the knowledge of what remains when the body is gone. Nothing is gained by wealth, Yama answers, and the path is the one the wise have called the choice of the good over the pleasant: the self is not born and does not die, it is not to be reached by argument but by the one who seeks it, and the senses must be held in the mind as horses in the reins.",
  "historicalContext": "The Upanishad from which the verse about the chariot and the rider comes, and among the most retold of the Vedanta texts; it was the first Upanishad to appear separately in English in the nineteenth century and is still the one most often taught first.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: the six vallis of the GRETIL e-text, streamed at read time one valli at a time. This is the Sanskrit text; the English translation held elsewhere in the library is a separate edition by a named translator.",
